(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-3-history-physical-documentation-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"History and Physical Documentation (Set 2) — Physical Diagnosis 1 Exam 3","category":"Physical Diagnosis","questions":[{"q":"In what order does the lecture say the physical examination should be documented?","choices":["Beginning with the system related to the chief complaint","In the order the findings happened to be discovered","Grouping all normal findings first, then all abnormal findings","Starting with the general survey and moving through all body systems"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture asks you to document what you observed, starting with the general survey and working through all body systems. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed in the lecture as a barrier to communication?","choices":["Documenting the encounter after the patient has left","Ordering diagnostic testing before the history is complete","Using a standardized rather than a real patient","Cultural differences between the clinician and the patient"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists emotional, sensory, cultural, disability and other health problems, language, and terminology as barriers. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What does the lecture's summary say about how different providers write notes?","choices":["Every provider must use an identical template without deviation","Not every provider writes notes the same way, but there is a basic format","Format is entirely a matter of personal style","Formats differ so much that comparison between notes is impossible"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs this with keeping things in the right order. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What belongs in the subjective portion of a progress note?","choices":["A complete retelling of the original history of present illness","Vital signs and updated laboratory results","Updates on patient comments or complaints since the previous note","The final diagnosis with supporting argument"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes updates on comments or complaints — changes, pain level, symptoms better, worse, resolved, or new — covering the interval since the last note. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which set of four areas does the lecture group under the mnemonic used to elicit the patient's perspective on their illness?","choices":["Frequency, intensity, family history, and examination findings","Feeling, ideas, function, and expectations","Findings, impression, formulation, and evaluation","Focus, insight, function, and emotional response"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines these as feeling (fears or concern), ideas (about nature and cause), function (how life has been affected), and expectations (of the illness, clinician, or health care). — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What should the first differential diagnosis listed be?","choices":["The one that would be most dangerous if it were missed","The one that is quickest to confirm with a bedside test","The one the patient believes is most likely to be the cause","The most likely"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture says the first diagnosis should be the most likely, explained using evidence from the history and physical examination. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which item does the lecture place in the past medical history?","choices":["Alcohol, nicotine and recreational drug use","The three-generation account of relatives' health","Occupation, travel, and support systems","Age-appropriate screenings and their results"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists major illnesses, surgeries, hospitalizations, childhood illnesses, immunizations, obstetric, gynecologic or genitourinary history where applicable, and age-appropriate screenings with results, all with specific dates and timelines. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What limitation does the lecture note about simulated or standardized patients?","choices":["Script and findings cannot exactly mimic the real patient experience","They cannot be used to practise the physical examination at all","Their histories are always internally inconsistent","They may not be used for graded assessments in the program"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture names tone of voice, body language, and things left unsaid as what a script cannot reproduce. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which units does the lecture specify for documenting vital signs on program assignments?","choices":["Metric","Imperial units, since they are more familiar to most patients","Whichever units the recording device happens to display","Both metric and imperial, recorded side by side for clarity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture asks for a bulleted list in metric units, and its sample uses centimetres, kilograms, millimetres of mercury, and degrees Celsius. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which problem does the lecture place under content issues rather than technical issues?","choices":["Word errors within an otherwise complete sentence","Inconsistency between two parts of the same note","Vague details such as calling a finding normal or appropriate","Information placed in the wrong section of the document"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Content issues cover not enough information, confusion between a history and physical and a progress note, vague terms such as normal, abnormal or appropriate, and incomplete descriptions of exam findings. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"For program assignments, where do vital signs belong and how should they be presented?","choices":["Within the history of present illness, written as prose","At the very end of the physical examination section, recorded in imperial units","In the assessment, alongside the final diagnosis","As the first element of the physical examination, as a bulleted list in metric units"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that for these program assignments vital signs come first in the physical examination and are best documented as a bulleted list using metric units. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about the pain scale in the history of present illness?","choices":["A score above seven automatically requires immediate escalation","Record the score and ask what a ten means for that patient","The scale should be replaced with a description in the patient's words","The score should be recorded only if the complaint is pain"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs the one-to-ten pain scale with the question of what a ten means for the patient, because the anchor differs between people. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What form should the assessment take?","choices":["A simple list, because the arguments belong in the differential statements","A narrative paragraph that re-argues the case for each diagnosis in turn","A ranked table of every symptom the patient reported","A copy of the chief complaint in the patient's own words"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes the assessment as a simple list, since all arguments for the diagnosis should already have been made in the differential statements. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"How should the differential diagnoses be ordered and justified?","choices":["Alphabetically, with testing listed separately in the plan","Most likely first, with evidence from the history and examination explaining why","Least likely first, so that the most serious possibility appears at the end of the list","In the order the symptoms were reported by the patient"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture asks for three or more, the first being most likely and justified from history and physical findings, with the others explained as considered but less likely. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"The lecture groups common problems into three categories. Which set is correct?","choices":["Legal, financial, and administrative issues","Technical, content, and organization issues","Grammar, spelling, and formatting issues","Timing, sequencing, and delegation issues"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Technical covers missing components, terminology, word errors and inconsistency; content covers insufficient information, vague details and incomplete descriptions; organization covers difficulty following the exam or the patient's story and information in the wrong section. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What does the lecture say a first encounter with a patient establishes?","choices":["The legal basis on which subsequent documentation rests","The tone for further visits","The sequence in which body systems will later be examined","The reliability rating that will be recorded in the identifying data"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture opens its history-taking section by saying the first encounter sets the tone for further visits, and stresses a good first impression. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which element does the lecture include in the identifying data at the top of a history and physical?","choices":["The working diagnosis at the time of arrival","The referring clinician's contact details","Reliability of the source of the history","A summary of the most recent laboratory results"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists name, sex, birth date, age, race or ethnic origin, source of history, reliability of that source, and the date and time of the history. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which technique does the lecture describe as repeating a patient's own words back to them?","choices":["Echoing","Signposting, which announces that the interview is moving to a different section","Validation, which conveys that the patient's emotional response is understandable","Summarization, which condenses the account so far and invites correction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists echoing alongside clarification under guided questioning as a way to invite the patient to continue in their own terms. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A patient describes chest discomfort and the clinician replies, \"So the tightness comes on when you climb stairs and settles when you rest — have I got that right?\" Which communication technique is this?","choices":["Summarization, which also shows the clinician was listening carefully","Echoing, which repeats the patient's last words back to them","Validation, which legitimises the emotions the patient reports","Signposting, which flags a change of topic before it happens"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes summarization as restating what has been heard; it demonstrates careful listening and helps identify what is known and what is still missing. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the relationship between the history and the physical examination?","choices":["The physical examination should be completed before any history is taken","The history alone is sufficient when the patient is a reliable source","They are taught separately but are intertwined, each guiding the other","They are documented as a single undivided narrative section"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states the history guides the physical examination while examination findings elicit further history questions. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What does the lecture instruct you to document in the physical examination section?","choices":["The conclusions you reached about each body system examined","Only those findings that differ from the expected normal","What you observed","The patient's own description of how each area felt to them"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture says to document what you observed, starting with the general survey and moving through all body systems. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A rectal examination is not performed because the patient declines. How does the lecture say this should appear in the note?","choices":["The section should be left blank so no claim is made","The section should be marked normal since nothing abnormal was seen","The section should be documented as not done, with an explanation","The section should be deleted entirely from the template"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives exactly this format, with examples such as patient refused, deferred by preceptor, or deferred per assignment instructions. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"In the lecture's sample vital signs, what detail accompanies the blood pressure reading?","choices":["The arm used and the patient's position","The cuff size and the device manufacturer","The time elapsed since the patient last stood","The reading from both arms averaged together"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The sample records blood pressure as 120/80 mm Hg right arm, seated. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"How does the lecture characterise the patient education element of the plan?","choices":["Detailed","Optional when the patient is already familiar with the condition","Limited to a single sentence for readability","Replaced by a printed handout in most encounters"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture writes patient education in the plan as DETAILED, and its worked example runs to several specific instructions covering expected timeline, side effects, fluids, hygiene, and when to call. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"The lecture gives a rule about abbreviations in the physical examination. What is it?","choices":["Abbreviations are preferred because they keep the note concise","Abbreviations are acceptable if defined once at the top of the note","Only abbreviations approved by the institution may be used","Avoid them — when in doubt, spell it out"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states this in the physical examination section and repeats it in the summary, warning that acronyms and abbreviations may cause misunderstandings and medical errors. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the review of systems?","choices":["A focused enquiry limited to the system named in the chief complaint","A head-to-toe survey of body systems and symptoms, covering each system in full detail","A restatement of the physical examination findings in the patient's words","A summary of the patient's previous specialist consultations"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. That is the lecture's description. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What does the lecture recommend before a return visit, where records are available?","choices":["Ask the patient to repeat the entire history again from the very beginning","Review the records and identify information needing update or clarification","Complete the note in advance from the previous entry","Delegate the update to another member of the team"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture recommends reviewing records before visits whenever possible and identifying anything needing to be updated or clarified, while maintaining focus on the patient so they feel heard. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which step does the lecture include among its suggested approach to developing differentials?","choices":["Choose the most probable diagnosis first, then collect the findings that support it","Defer all hypotheses until every laboratory result has returned","Restrict consideration to problems within a single organ system","Identify abnormal findings, consider their anatomic location, and look for patterns"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists identifying abnormal findings, considering anatomic location, looking for patterns, seeking probable cause, generating hypotheses, and then testing them. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about the structure of a patient interview?","choices":["Structure matters, but be flexible and adapt to the patient's needs","A fixed sequence should be followed identically for every patient","Structure should be abandoned entirely in favour of free conversation","The patient should be given a written form to complete first"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states there is no perfect formula for every patient, that structure is important but not everything, and that the clinician should adapt. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"How should the chief complaint be recorded?","choices":["As a working diagnosis chosen by the clinician on arrival","As a summary of the review of systems in clinical terms","In the patient's own words, together with a duration","As a list of the abnormal findings noted on examination"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture requires the patient's own words with duration, explaining why the patient sought care and how long the problem has been present. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"}]};
